{"reference":"1 Corinthians 4","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Judgment, humility, and status","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Paul calls ministers stewards whose faithfulness is finally assessed by the Lord. His warning to “judge nothing before the time” cautions against premature final verdicts about hidden motives; it does not make leaders answerable only to themselves or suspend accountability for abuse.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 4:1-5","1 Corinthians 4:8-21"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_004_6f933ab2b1f6fa491dbe4289"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter’s sharp contrast is deliberate: the Corinthians are portrayed as already full, rich, wise, strong, and honored, while the apostles appear as a public spectacle—weak, dishonored, hungry, exposed, beaten, unsettled, and laboring. This exposes claims that status or comfort establish superior spiritual credentials. At the same time, Paul’s appeal to imitate him does not require reproducing deprivation, abuse, or untreated harm.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 4:1-5","1 Corinthians 4:6-7","1 Corinthians 4:8-13","1 Corinthians 4:6-21"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_004_5b7aa8899e8145817e150691","reader_path_1_corinthians_004_597048a5cd17c835859449eb"]}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Correction and family language","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul describes his correction in family terms: he calls the Corinthians beloved children, says he became their father through the good news, urges them to imitate him, and sends Timothy to remind them of his ways. The language emphasizes relational admonition and delegated teaching, but it should not be treated as ownership, secrecy, immunity from scrutiny, or a demand for unquestioning loyalty.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 4:14-21","1 Corinthians 4:6-7"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_004_ae9de0c646158fd89f47c965"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The letter’s transmission","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Manuscripts preserving 1 Corinthians contain different portions and variants; no single surviving witness simply is the original copy. The letter is therefore read through its transmitted textual witnesses, while proposals that divide or remove parts of it remain matters to be assessed passage by passage.","confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_004_c1a8a51c1b0175cf1f793d6b"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}